5. libgcc (GPL-3.0-or-later WITH GCC-exception-3.1)
----------------------------------------------------

Both programs were compiled by GCC 15.2.0 (Alpine's gcc), which links small
runtime routines from libgcc into the programs it builds. libgcc is licensed under the GNU GPL
version 3 or later with the GCC Runtime Library Exception 3.1, which allows
the resulting programs to be distributed under terms of the author's choice:
https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gcc-exception-3.1.html. GCC's source is at
https://gcc.gnu.org/, and Alpine's build of it in aports main/gcc.


6. payload/ (the install zip only)
----------------------------------

pmos-amazon-biscuit-rNNN.zip also carries payload/boot.img and
payload/pmos.img.xz: a postmarketOS system for the Echo, built with
pmbootstrap from Alpine Linux, postmarketOS and this project's packages. Each
package in it keeps its own licence. The installed system lists every package
in /lib/apk/db/installed: its exact version (the "V:" line), its licence (the
"L:" line) and, for Alpine's packages, the aports commit it was built from
(the "c:" line). The corresponding source of each is:

  Alpine packages    the package's directory in Alpine's aports at that
                     commit, and the source files its APKBUILD names:
                     https://gitlab.alpinelinux.org/alpine/aports
  postmarketOS       the APKBUILD for that version in postmarketOS's
  packages           pmaports, and the source files it names:
                     https://gitlab.postmarketos.org/postmarketOS/pmaports
  this project's     https://github.com/liamtw22/pmaports-biscuit at tag
  packages           rNNN: device-amazon-biscuit and its subpackages,
                     linux-amazon-biscuit (the Linux kernel, GPL-2.0) and its
                     patches, and the patched boot-deploy 0.24.0-r1
                     (GPL-2.0-or-later) in base-pmaports/. The kernel is also
                     a git tree, in https://github.com/liamtw22/linux-mtk
                     (branch biscuit-r<kernel release>).

The same GitHub release as the zips carries the complete source of the
kernel in the image, as linux-amazon-biscuit-r<kernel release>-source.tar.gz,
and of the BusyBox in bin/busybox (section 1).

No Amazon files are in any zip. The firmware, microphone files, speaker
correction curve, sounds and light ring animations the Echo uses are copied
from the owner's own Echo by amazon-biscuit-backup, and stay on that Echo and
on the owner's computer.
